unmerged commits).\n\n**What I did:** Threaded `repo.DefaultBranch` into `commitsOn` and `Reconcile`; added a fail-closed guard so a leftover branch is only ever judged when the default branch is actually known (never guessed as \"empty\"), while a repo with nothing to sweep needs no answer at all. Added `resolveDefaultBranches(*Roster)` in `main.go`, called once at the top of `doRun` before the sweep, reusing the same `gh` call `reconcileBoards` already made every invocation rather than adding a new one. `ReleaseStranded`/`strand.go` (fw-tf4) isn't in this tree yet (PR #80 still open), so it's untouched — flagged in the PR/bead for when it lands.\n\n**Review:** Ran `/flywheel-review` (3 parallel lenses). Correctness and security: no blocking findings. Simplicity: caught that `resolveDefaultBranches` mutated through a value-receiver `Roster`, hiding a real side effect — fixed to take `*Roster`. A minor duplication note was rejected (different failure policies). All 4 findings recorded in the ledger.\n\n**Gate:** `go build`, `go vet`, `go test ./...` clean; full pre-commit hook (identity, replay-guard, gofmt, beads, vet, vet-conformance, test) passed on all three commits.\n\n**PR:** [#84](https://github.com/jamesponwith/agentic-flywheel/pull/84), `w:1`. Bead left open per protocol — not merging, human is merge authority.\n\n**Abandoned:** nothing. Minor housekeeping left behind: a stray `tools/fleet/fleet` build binary in the worktree that the sandbox wouldn't let me `rm` — harmless, untracked, not staged.","ttft_ms":2239,"type":"result","duration_ms":788403,"uuid":"8d373f15-7367-4478-bf41-afdb7cd3f08f","ttft_stream_ms":954,"time_to_request_ms":151,"first_content_frame_ms":1331,"queued_turn_count":0}
